     "type": "text",
     "text": "{\"count\": 2, \"rationale\": \"Deploy mfa:admin and mfa:dbadmin. Every exfiltration path needs a foothold on db, fileserver or backup. Each of those auth techniques requires NOT mfa_dbadmin OR admin_dc OR admin_adfs. With mfa_dbadmin deployed, the attacker would need admin_dc or admin_adfs. Those need foothold_dc or foothold_adfs, whose auth techniques require NOT mfa_admin. So with both MFA controls deployed, no db, fileserver or backup foothold is reachable, even though the attacker can still reach internal, steal admin credentials via kerberoast and get stolen_dbadmin via ciserver. No single control is enough. mfa:dbadmin alone fails because the dc path (stolen_admin, then auth_dc, escalate_dc, admin_dc) unlocks auth_db. mfa:admin alone fails because admin_ciserver leaks stolen_dbadmin and auth_db is then open. segmented alone fails because phishing plus an unpatched escalate_workstation gives admin_workstation, which satisfies pivot_internal. Patches and mfa:user alone fail because phished workstation footholds bypass mfa_user, and stolen_admin bypasses server patches. Therefore the minimum is 2.\"}"
    }
